<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monothelitism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Monotheletism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monothelitism was the Christian doctrine that Christ had two natures but only one will. Promoted by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> in his Ecthesis of 638 as a compromise to reconcile opponents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a>, it was rejected by the popes and by Maximus the Confessor and caused a long dispute between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The Third Council of Constantinople condemned it in 680–681.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Monothelitism grew out of earlier disputes over the nature of Christ. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> defined Christ as having two natures in one hypostasis, while the non-Chalcedonians held that he had one nature. Since one nature seemed to imply one will, Eastern Roman rulers in the seventh century hoped a doctrine of one will would unite the quarrelling parties. Many provinces later lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> were non-Chalcedonian: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> about 30,000 Chalcedonian Greeks faced some five million Coptic non-Chalcedonians.</p><p>The first attempt was monoenergism, the teaching of one energy. Patriarch Sergius I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> promoted it with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, who by 622 had written of Christ's single energy. By 630 three of the five patriarchates taught one theandric energy. A monk named Sophronius opposed it, became Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 634 and attacked it as a disguised form of monophysitism. Support faded and Sergius and Heraclius dropped it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sergius then devised a new formula, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> issued as the Ecthesis in 638. It banned talk of one or two energies and declared that Christ had two natures but a single will, which could never oppose the divine will. The eastern patriarchs accepted it, but Pope Severinus and Pope John IV rejected it, producing a schism between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Heraclius, old and ill, said on his deathbed that Sergius had pressured him into approving it.</p><p>Maximus the Confessor led the opposition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Constans II, who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, issued the Type of Constans, which forbade any discussion of one or two wills. Pope Martin I, at the Lateran Council of 649, condemned both the Ecthesis and the Type. The exarch Theodore I Calliopas seized Martin and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he was imprisoned, tortured and banished to Chersonesus, where he soon died. Maximus lost his tongue and right hand.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After Constans died in 668, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-iv">Constantine IV</a> left the question to a church council. Pope Agatho held a synod at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 680, and synods in the West condemned the doctrine. The Third Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, meeting in 680 and 681, condemned monothelitism and affirmed that Christ had two natural wills and two natural energies. It also anathematised Pope Honorius I, a point later debated in discussions of papal infallibility; Leo II's letter read the censure as aimed at his silence rather than at error of belief. The Oriental Orthodox deny that they ever held the doctrine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Monothelitism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Eugene I was chosen bishop of Rome after Emperor Constans II had Martin I arrested, carried off to Constantinople and banished to Cherson for his strong opposition to <b>Monothelitism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-10">10 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He wrote to the clergy of Ireland and Scotland about their mistakes in the time of keeping Easter, and he condemned <b>Monothelitism</b> as heresy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-24">24 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Vitalian became bishop of Rome after the death of Eugene I, amid the dispute between the papacy and Constantinople over <b>Monothelitism</b>, which Rome condemned.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-30">30 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Pope Martin I, an opponent of <b>Monothelitism</b>, was arrested in the Lateran in Rome with Maximus the Confessor on the orders of Emperor Constans II and taken to Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-17">17 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Relations with the popes were often tense, especially over <b>Monothelitism</b> and Iconoclasm.</blockquote><a href="/state/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a></li></ul></section>
